WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:06.120
OLIVIA MORGAN: Hi, guys.

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:10.320
I'm Olivia Morgan, and
I'm a Looker CE at Google.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:12.000
And today's session
is going to be

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:15.060
a technical deep dive on
Looker, the enterprise BI

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:18.940
solution for Google Cloud.

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:21.370
To start off with a
lightweight agenda,

00:00:21.370 --> 00:00:23.500
we're going to go
over three key pieces.

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:27.580
I want to start with a
Looker architecture overview.

00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:29.560
Then we'll touch on
some of the key points

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:32.619
that I'll be going over in
today's technical deep dive.

00:00:32.619 --> 00:00:34.360
And we'll spend
most of the session

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:37.490
on the technical
deep dive itself.

00:00:37.490 --> 00:00:39.850
And so to kick off the
Looker architecture overview,

00:00:39.850 --> 00:00:42.640
I wanted to start
with a statement

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:46.880
that explains the BI landscape
and how we experience data.

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:49.540
And so what this says
is how we experience

00:00:49.540 --> 00:00:51.520
data is not what it used to be.

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:54.640
An effective dashboard is
great, but the Looker platform

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:57.300
opens up a world
of possibilities.

00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:59.950
And by large and in
part, the Looker platform

00:00:59.950 --> 00:01:02.530
opens up these possibilities
because of the way

00:01:02.530 --> 00:01:04.340
that we're architected
and because

00:01:04.340 --> 00:01:07.780
of the way that we can sit on
top of a SQL database or a SQL

00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:09.168
warehouse.

00:01:09.168 --> 00:01:11.210
So if you look at this
slide, at the very bottom,

00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:13.760
you're going to see a bunch
of different data sources,

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:17.170
things like Google
Ads, Shopify, HubSpot.

00:01:17.170 --> 00:01:20.290
And all of those sources are
pushed into your data warehouse

00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:21.890
or your data lake.

00:01:21.890 --> 00:01:25.180
Now, Looker, as a
BI platform, sits

00:01:25.180 --> 00:01:28.000
on top of that database
or data warehouse,

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:31.000
and it speaks SQL to that
database or data warehouse.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:32.470
And in this case,
in today's demo,

00:01:32.470 --> 00:01:34.860
we'll be sitting
on top of BigQuery.

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:37.510
And you can think of
Looker as a powerful API

00:01:37.510 --> 00:01:40.420
layer that brings a lot
of different benefits

00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:42.370
to the surface
for your end users

00:01:42.370 --> 00:01:44.890
and for your technical users.

00:01:44.890 --> 00:01:47.260
One of the key pieces
here is that we have

00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:49.370
governed metrics in Looker.

00:01:49.370 --> 00:01:51.910
So being that we're sitting
right on top of the database,

00:01:51.910 --> 00:01:55.630
Looker offers a one-stop shop
version controlled modeling

00:01:55.630 --> 00:01:58.030
layer, where you're defining
your business logic,

00:01:58.030 --> 00:02:00.000
and you're iterating
on that business logic

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.200
in a version controlled way.

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:05.890
We also offer
best-in-class APIs.

00:02:05.890 --> 00:02:09.830
So we not only have
API endpoints for data,

00:02:09.830 --> 00:02:14.710
we also have API endpoints for
managing your Looker instance,

00:02:14.710 --> 00:02:19.310
managing users, groups, or user
attributes for those users.

00:02:19.310 --> 00:02:24.070
We also have powerful API
SDKs and embed SDKs, if you're

00:02:24.070 --> 00:02:27.340
building custom applications.

00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:29.680
Really, a core tenant
of Looker and the way

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:32.650
that it's architected is
that it's in database.

00:02:32.650 --> 00:02:35.380
So one analogy I like to
use is, when you put Looker

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:38.500
on top of your database,
it's like chipping your car.

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:41.270
Whatever the engine of
that car is-- in this case,

00:02:41.270 --> 00:02:43.080
we're sitting on
top of BigQuery.

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:46.870
Looker is enhancing all of the
features and functionalities

00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:48.580
from that database
and will benefit

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:51.460
from any features and
functionality that then

00:02:51.460 --> 00:02:54.205
is brought upon that database.

00:02:54.205 --> 00:02:55.830
So on top of those
features, of course,

00:02:55.830 --> 00:02:57.130
we're version controlled.

00:02:57.130 --> 00:02:59.350
That allows for
additional security.

00:02:59.350 --> 00:03:01.240
And we are cloud
first, so Looker

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:03.050
does take a
multi-cloud approach,

00:03:03.050 --> 00:03:05.960
whether it's GCP
or any other cloud.

00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:07.870
And from there,
we're able to operate

00:03:07.870 --> 00:03:09.850
in four different
modes, if you're

00:03:09.850 --> 00:03:12.250
thinking of the BI
in different ways

00:03:12.250 --> 00:03:14.740
that you can serve these
analytics to different end

00:03:14.740 --> 00:03:16.220
users.

00:03:16.220 --> 00:03:18.940
The first one on the
left is, in the red box,

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:20.740
Modern BI and Analytics.

00:03:20.740 --> 00:03:24.190
And that's your traditional
BI data consumption--

00:03:24.190 --> 00:03:25.990
your reports, your dashboards.

00:03:25.990 --> 00:03:29.830
A use case there might be that
you're building a customer 360

00:03:29.830 --> 00:03:35.670
dashboard to view customers
across your channels and media.

00:03:35.670 --> 00:03:38.440
The green box,
Integrated Insights,

00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:40.810
is more along the lines of
bringing the data to where

00:03:40.810 --> 00:03:42.860
your users already consume it.

00:03:42.860 --> 00:03:45.850
So if you have sales folks
that operate in Salesforce,

00:03:45.850 --> 00:03:48.880
don't ask them to log
into another application.

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:52.750
Use the application to embed
those insights into the tools

00:03:52.750 --> 00:03:55.890
they already know and love.

00:03:55.890 --> 00:03:58.810
The yellow box,
Data-driven Workflows--

00:03:58.810 --> 00:04:01.350
this essentially automates
the first two parts.

00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:03.570
The idea is that your
BI tool is going to help

00:04:03.570 --> 00:04:05.370
you uncover those insights.

00:04:05.370 --> 00:04:08.430
So Looker will help you
uncover, perhaps, customers that

00:04:08.430 --> 00:04:12.360
are close to churning, based
off of a customer health score.

00:04:12.360 --> 00:04:14.190
And then within the
Looker application,

00:04:14.190 --> 00:04:16.890
you can use that application
to actually trigger

00:04:16.890 --> 00:04:18.540
the action you'd like to take--

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:20.790
maybe based off of a low
customer health score,

00:04:20.790 --> 00:04:24.540
we're sending emails
to those customers.

00:04:24.540 --> 00:04:27.450
And then the last one,
Custom Applications--

00:04:27.450 --> 00:04:29.670
a custom application
you can spin up

00:04:29.670 --> 00:04:31.260
in any way, shape, or form.

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:34.590
A large reason why Looker opens
this up in such a powerful way

00:04:34.590 --> 00:04:36.300
is because, not only
we're in-database,

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:39.060
but we do have a
powerful suite of APIs.

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:41.490
So when you have a version
controlled and governed layer

00:04:41.490 --> 00:04:43.770
to pull metrics
from, you can create

00:04:43.770 --> 00:04:45.480
a slew of custom applications.

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:49.860
You can use our APIs to render
visualizations or data points

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:54.380
to make any sort of custom
workflow you can dream up.

00:04:54.380 --> 00:04:56.450
Now, for the demo
today, I wanted

00:04:56.450 --> 00:04:58.400
to go over a few key
points of the pieces

00:04:58.400 --> 00:04:59.660
that I wanted to touch on.

00:04:59.660 --> 00:05:02.540
I really want to highlight
the fact that this instance is

00:05:02.540 --> 00:05:04.670
hosted on GCP, and
we are going to be

00:05:04.670 --> 00:05:06.643
sitting on top of BigQuery.

00:05:06.643 --> 00:05:08.060
So what you'll see
within the demo

00:05:08.060 --> 00:05:09.685
is that we're going
to use Google Cloud

00:05:09.685 --> 00:05:12.830
Functions and the search API
for things like product image

00:05:12.830 --> 00:05:13.850
searches.

00:05:13.850 --> 00:05:17.480
You'll notice that we have BQML
laced within our Looker models

00:05:17.480 --> 00:05:20.810
for item sales and
stock forecasting.

00:05:20.810 --> 00:05:22.310
We're also going
to use the BigQuery

00:05:22.310 --> 00:05:24.120
weather public data set.

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:25.670
So you'll see
examples where we can

00:05:25.670 --> 00:05:27.590
start to interact
with public data sets

00:05:27.590 --> 00:05:30.090
to make the analysis richer.

00:05:30.090 --> 00:05:32.060
We're also leveraging
nested tables

00:05:32.060 --> 00:05:34.340
for things like faster
performance on transaction

00:05:34.340 --> 00:05:36.320
analysis, in this case.

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:39.320
And then lastly,
we're able to utilize

00:05:39.320 --> 00:05:40.775
faster and more
efficient queries

00:05:40.775 --> 00:05:44.810
with BI engine and Looker's
aggregate awareness.

00:05:44.810 --> 00:05:47.900
So now I'd like to transition
into the technical deep dive,

00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:52.100
where I can show you Looker
on top of GCP and BigQuery.

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:55.460
In the Looker demonstration,
we are hosted on GCP,

00:05:55.460 --> 00:05:58.160
and we're sitting on top
of a BigQuery database.

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:01.010
We're working off of
a retail data set.

00:06:01.010 --> 00:06:04.340
And so our goal with our models
and view files and our LookML

00:06:04.340 --> 00:06:07.010
is to be able to support
end user experiences,

00:06:07.010 --> 00:06:11.120
like the merchandising
VP or regional VP logging

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:14.470
in to look at performance.

00:06:14.470 --> 00:06:16.470
Most of our time today
is going to be spent here

00:06:16.470 --> 00:06:18.070
in this Develop tab.

00:06:18.070 --> 00:06:20.250
But what we'll do is we'll
start in the Admin tab

00:06:20.250 --> 00:06:22.620
by creating a
database connection.

00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:25.830
So once that database
connection is created,

00:06:25.830 --> 00:06:28.470
the Develop tab is where we
build our Looker Symantec

00:06:28.470 --> 00:06:31.210
model, or the LookML.

00:06:31.210 --> 00:06:33.580
For those of us that are
developing in LookML,

00:06:33.580 --> 00:06:37.780
we're exposing those models to
end users via this Explore tab.

00:06:37.780 --> 00:06:39.310
And for those end
users, whatever

00:06:39.310 --> 00:06:41.020
they're building
in this Explore tab

00:06:41.020 --> 00:06:44.620
then populates the Browse
tab, which then also populates

00:06:44.620 --> 00:06:49.900
these boards and
folders for end users.

00:06:49.900 --> 00:06:51.730
Now, starting in
the Admin panel,

00:06:51.730 --> 00:06:55.360
I want to take you all to
our database connections

00:06:55.360 --> 00:06:57.010
and establish a
database connection.

00:06:57.010 --> 00:06:58.385
And we'll open
this in a new tab.

00:07:00.553 --> 00:07:02.720
Now, when you're establishing
a database connection,

00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:05.137
you can go ahead and add the
new connection button up here

00:07:05.137 --> 00:07:06.080
at the top.

00:07:06.080 --> 00:07:07.880
For our demo, we already
have a connection

00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:09.608
established with Looker data.

00:07:09.608 --> 00:07:11.900
So I'm going to go ahead and
edit this connection so we

00:07:11.900 --> 00:07:15.670
can see how this is set up.

00:07:15.670 --> 00:07:17.970
So we have our Looker
data connection.

00:07:17.970 --> 00:07:20.820
The dialect we are connected
to, as I mentioned, is BigQuery.

00:07:20.820 --> 00:07:22.350
But if I open up
this panel, you can

00:07:22.350 --> 00:07:24.253
see all of the
different dialects--

00:07:24.253 --> 00:07:25.920
or a variety of the
different dialects--

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:27.710
that Looker connects to.

00:07:27.710 --> 00:07:31.500
On the Google front, we've got
Legacy, Standard, PostgreSQL--

00:07:31.500 --> 00:07:35.760
and also Cloud SQL
and Cloud Spanner.

00:07:35.760 --> 00:07:38.340
Once we've selected
the dialect, you

00:07:38.340 --> 00:07:40.530
can select the project
name and data set name

00:07:40.530 --> 00:07:42.190
that you're pointing Looker to.

00:07:42.190 --> 00:07:43.980
But let's say your
use case calls

00:07:43.980 --> 00:07:45.840
for a little bit more dynamism.

00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:48.300
You can also use user
attributes in Looker

00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:52.410
to dynamically set a value
for a user or a group,

00:07:52.410 --> 00:07:55.100
if you're pointing them to
separate project names or data

00:07:55.100 --> 00:07:57.310
sets.

00:07:57.310 --> 00:08:00.930
Now, within Google, many
of you might use OAuth.

00:08:00.930 --> 00:08:03.420
And so Looker does
integrate with OAuth.

00:08:03.420 --> 00:08:06.540
And we can use a OAuth
for IM integration.

00:08:06.540 --> 00:08:08.393
We support service accounts.

00:08:08.393 --> 00:08:09.810
So that's another
way that you can

00:08:09.810 --> 00:08:12.120
support authentication
and connection

00:08:12.120 --> 00:08:16.000
settings within Looker.

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:17.835
Add your usernames
and your passwords.

00:08:17.835 --> 00:08:19.210
And then down here
at the bottom,

00:08:19.210 --> 00:08:22.030
another interesting feature here
is that max billing gigabyte.

00:08:22.030 --> 00:08:24.960
So if you wanted to set
a maximum number of bytes

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:28.350
billed allowed on a
single BigQuery query,

00:08:28.350 --> 00:08:29.230
you could do that.

00:08:29.230 --> 00:08:31.990
And then similar to those host
and ports up there at the top,

00:08:31.990 --> 00:08:35.010
if we wanted to dynamically set
this value for users or groups,

00:08:35.010 --> 00:08:37.720
we could.

00:08:37.720 --> 00:08:41.049
Now that the connection's been
established, what we need to do

00:08:41.049 --> 00:08:44.800
is we need to create
a LookML project.

00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:46.810
Now, the LookML
projects in Looker

00:08:46.810 --> 00:08:48.850
are essentially a
house that contains

00:08:48.850 --> 00:08:51.850
all of your model
and your view files.

00:08:51.850 --> 00:08:54.580
Before we move into that
project, what we'll want to do

00:08:54.580 --> 00:08:57.520
is jump into development mode.

00:08:57.520 --> 00:08:59.800
And this allows
me, as a developer,

00:08:59.800 --> 00:09:03.700
to start editing or
creating my own projects.

00:09:03.700 --> 00:09:05.680
So to walk you guys
through the flow,

00:09:05.680 --> 00:09:08.560
I'm going to click on
Manage LookML Projects,

00:09:08.560 --> 00:09:11.410
and I'll show you how
to create a new project.

00:09:11.410 --> 00:09:13.030
Up in the right-hand
corner, we'll

00:09:13.030 --> 00:09:16.037
select New LookML Project.

00:09:16.037 --> 00:09:17.870
And what we'll do is
we can give our project

00:09:17.870 --> 00:09:21.440
a name, a starting
point, and a connection.

00:09:21.440 --> 00:09:24.130
So we can name the project
whatever we'd like.

00:09:24.130 --> 00:09:25.570
The starting point
really depends

00:09:25.570 --> 00:09:28.360
on how you would like
to begin building

00:09:28.360 --> 00:09:31.340
the logic for your analysis.

00:09:31.340 --> 00:09:33.430
So in many cases, folks
will generate a model

00:09:33.430 --> 00:09:34.840
from a database schema.

00:09:34.840 --> 00:09:36.940
In this case, Looker
scans the database

00:09:36.940 --> 00:09:39.490
and brings back or automatically
generates the models

00:09:39.490 --> 00:09:41.890
in the view files.

00:09:41.890 --> 00:09:46.450
We can select generate from SQL,
clone a public Git repository--

00:09:46.450 --> 00:09:50.630
which this demo is based
off of one of our blocks.

00:09:50.630 --> 00:09:53.350
So if you decided to point
Looker to one of our public Git

00:09:53.350 --> 00:09:56.170
repos that we have hosted
for a particular source

00:09:56.170 --> 00:10:00.430
or analytic analysis,
we can point Looker

00:10:00.430 --> 00:10:03.370
to that repository, and it will
automatically generate or pull

00:10:03.370 --> 00:10:07.020
those files for us as well.

00:10:07.020 --> 00:10:09.980
Now, in our case, we already
have a project defined.

00:10:09.980 --> 00:10:11.600
So in the Development
tab, I want

00:10:11.600 --> 00:10:14.570
to navigate you guys
to the retail demo.

00:10:17.860 --> 00:10:20.558
And within the
retail data set, what

00:10:20.558 --> 00:10:22.600
you'll see here in the
upper left-hand corner is,

00:10:22.600 --> 00:10:24.010
we are version controlled.

00:10:24.010 --> 00:10:25.870
So when I jumped into
development mode,

00:10:25.870 --> 00:10:29.260
I essentially put myself
into my own code branch.

00:10:29.260 --> 00:10:31.960
We have look LookML validation
here on the left side

00:10:31.960 --> 00:10:35.710
as well before you push
changes to production.

00:10:35.710 --> 00:10:37.690
But the main components
I want to focus on here

00:10:37.690 --> 00:10:39.370
are models and views.

00:10:39.370 --> 00:10:42.250
So you'll see we have a model
folder and a view folder that

00:10:42.250 --> 00:10:45.900
contains a variety
of different views.

00:10:45.900 --> 00:10:50.020
For Looker, a model file points
to a database connection.

00:10:50.020 --> 00:10:52.890
So here, we've pointed it to
our Looker data connection.

00:10:52.890 --> 00:10:55.710
And within this connection,
what the file tells

00:10:55.710 --> 00:11:00.750
Looker is how to think about
the schema of that database.

00:11:00.750 --> 00:11:03.240
And so in this case, we
have our transactions table

00:11:03.240 --> 00:11:08.620
or transaction view file right
after an explore parameter.

00:11:08.620 --> 00:11:11.670
Now, the file name that comes
after the explore parameter

00:11:11.670 --> 00:11:14.310
populates the from
clause in the SQL query.

00:11:14.310 --> 00:11:17.050
And so the way we read
this is from transactions,

00:11:17.050 --> 00:11:20.760
join transaction line items,
join customers, join customer

00:11:20.760 --> 00:11:24.300
facts, and so on and so forth.

00:11:24.300 --> 00:11:27.390
When we're establishing
join relationships,

00:11:27.390 --> 00:11:31.260
we use the relationship
parameter and the SQL parameter

00:11:31.260 --> 00:11:34.380
to help Looker protect
against symmetric aggregates.

00:11:34.380 --> 00:11:36.450
So once we define
these parameters,

00:11:36.450 --> 00:11:39.750
Looker will automatically
detect over-counting,

00:11:39.750 --> 00:11:46.140
and it will adjust to protect
from any symmetric aggregates.

00:11:46.140 --> 00:11:48.290
Now, in this case, we
have a transactions table,

00:11:48.290 --> 00:11:50.220
and we have a
transaction line items.

00:11:50.220 --> 00:11:52.440
This is nested data
within BigQuery.

00:11:52.440 --> 00:11:55.710
And so Looker works
seamlessly with nested data.

00:11:55.710 --> 00:11:57.480
All we have to do in
the SQL parameter,

00:11:57.480 --> 00:12:00.870
or what Looker will do when it
detects a nested field, is it

00:12:00.870 --> 00:12:02.910
will do a left join unnest--

00:12:02.910 --> 00:12:06.000
in this case, on our
line items table--

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:08.280
so that we can start to
use this for analysis

00:12:08.280 --> 00:12:11.470
and unpack that data.

00:12:11.470 --> 00:12:15.570
Now, another important thing
here is our always filter.

00:12:15.570 --> 00:12:18.190
In Looker, you can
use always filter

00:12:18.190 --> 00:12:21.610
to apply a filter restriction
on this explore section,

00:12:21.610 --> 00:12:24.910
so that when users are running
queries, they're protected,

00:12:24.910 --> 00:12:29.700
A, against running large
historical queries, but B,

00:12:29.700 --> 00:12:33.920
that we are pointing them
to a BigQuery partition.

00:12:33.920 --> 00:12:36.340
So if we actually go
to the Explore section

00:12:36.340 --> 00:12:40.160
and we type in our retail,
our transaction detail

00:12:40.160 --> 00:12:44.290
explore right here,
you'll notice, at the top,

00:12:44.290 --> 00:12:46.180
we have a filter for
transaction date.

00:12:46.180 --> 00:12:47.860
And it is required.

00:12:47.860 --> 00:12:50.890
So no matter what I pull-- if I
go here to the left-hand side,

00:12:50.890 --> 00:12:53.650
and I decide to
pull channel name,

00:12:53.650 --> 00:12:56.870
that SQL parameter
will always have

00:12:56.870 --> 00:13:00.702
a where clause pointing us
to either the partition time

00:13:00.702 --> 00:13:01.910
or the partition in BigQuery.

00:13:05.450 --> 00:13:09.420
Now, going back to
our model file--

00:13:09.420 --> 00:13:11.850
if our model is where we point
to the database connection

00:13:11.850 --> 00:13:15.140
and we explain to
Looker the schema,

00:13:15.140 --> 00:13:18.400
the view files are how
we define the tables.

00:13:18.400 --> 00:13:20.760
Now, you'll notice, when
we spun up the project,

00:13:20.760 --> 00:13:24.220
Looker will automatically
create a list of views for us.

00:13:24.220 --> 00:13:26.347
Let's say there are views
that I would like to add.

00:13:26.347 --> 00:13:27.930
There's two different
ways that we can

00:13:27.930 --> 00:13:29.850
start to add additional files.

00:13:29.850 --> 00:13:31.830
I can go ahead and
create another model,

00:13:31.830 --> 00:13:35.130
if I'd like to point to
another database connection.

00:13:35.130 --> 00:13:39.720
Or I can create views from
table or create views outright.

00:13:39.720 --> 00:13:42.870
If I create a view from table,
what that workflow looks like,

00:13:42.870 --> 00:13:45.060
as I'm iterating in
my project, Looker

00:13:45.060 --> 00:13:47.610
will bring you to
a schema viewer

00:13:47.610 --> 00:13:50.310
where I can simply
select a schema.

00:13:50.310 --> 00:13:51.360
I can select a table.

00:13:51.360 --> 00:13:53.950
Maybe I'm selecting
my Zillow data here.

00:13:53.950 --> 00:13:56.730
And at the bottom, I
can bring these files

00:13:56.730 --> 00:14:00.480
or create views, which will
create a new view file here

00:14:00.480 --> 00:14:01.065
in my project.

00:14:06.660 --> 00:14:10.890
Now, for the view files in
this demonstration today,

00:14:10.890 --> 00:14:14.870
we have a few base views and a
few examples of derived tables

00:14:14.870 --> 00:14:16.850
or derived views in Looker.

00:14:16.850 --> 00:14:19.820
So if I open up
my views dropdown,

00:14:19.820 --> 00:14:22.160
a couple of the core
tables that we have here

00:14:22.160 --> 00:14:26.330
are things like our transactions
table, our store dimension

00:14:26.330 --> 00:14:30.020
table, our customer
Dim, our product Dim.

00:14:30.020 --> 00:14:34.340
If I look at our base
transactions table,

00:14:34.340 --> 00:14:37.620
you can see the view name
and the SQL table name,

00:14:37.620 --> 00:14:41.090
which points us to that table
sitting in the database,

00:14:41.090 --> 00:14:43.270
the schema dot table name.

00:14:43.270 --> 00:14:45.530
And every column coming
from the database

00:14:45.530 --> 00:14:47.360
is now its own dimension.

00:14:47.360 --> 00:14:49.670
Now, you'll notice,
underneath each dimension,

00:14:49.670 --> 00:14:52.100
we have a couple
different parameters.

00:14:52.100 --> 00:14:53.870
In this case, we've
got a type number.

00:14:53.870 --> 00:14:55.880
This is the primary key.

00:14:55.880 --> 00:14:58.490
And in the SQL parameter,
it's pointing Looker

00:14:58.490 --> 00:15:01.800
to table dot transaction ID.

00:15:01.800 --> 00:15:04.820
Now, this table is injecting
that single table name,

00:15:04.820 --> 00:15:08.430
retail demo dot transaction
detail dot transaction ID.

00:15:11.260 --> 00:15:14.110
Now, this injection
becomes very important

00:15:14.110 --> 00:15:18.010
when we are deriving
new fields in Looker.

00:15:18.010 --> 00:15:21.310
So if we come down here
to our derived dimensions,

00:15:21.310 --> 00:15:24.380
you'll notice a couple
different examples.

00:15:24.380 --> 00:15:26.480
One example is a
dimension group.

00:15:26.480 --> 00:15:29.620
So we've got since first
customer transaction.

00:15:29.620 --> 00:15:31.750
What we're doing is we've
derived a new field.

00:15:31.750 --> 00:15:36.020
In this case, it's a mentioned
group of type duration.

00:15:36.020 --> 00:15:38.240
And we've given
Looker a SQL start

00:15:38.240 --> 00:15:44.060
and a SQL end date to derive the
months since our customer first

00:15:44.060 --> 00:15:46.310
transacted.

00:15:46.310 --> 00:15:47.990
Now, why this is
significant is, instead

00:15:47.990 --> 00:15:50.450
of pointing to a table dot
column name, a field that

00:15:50.450 --> 00:15:54.830
exists in the database, I'm
pointing us to another field

00:15:54.830 --> 00:15:57.020
in order to derive a new field.

00:15:57.020 --> 00:16:01.620
And so SQL is a
declarative language,

00:16:01.620 --> 00:16:03.260
but it doesn't always
lend itself well

00:16:03.260 --> 00:16:05.840
to the benefits of
object-oriented programming.

00:16:05.840 --> 00:16:08.300
And so this is an example
where LookML really

00:16:08.300 --> 00:16:11.750
brings in those values of
object oriented programming,

00:16:11.750 --> 00:16:15.960
in the sense of reusability
and extensibility.

00:16:15.960 --> 00:16:18.270
So when I continue
to scroll down here,

00:16:18.270 --> 00:16:20.130
and I'm looking at my measures--

00:16:20.130 --> 00:16:23.160
number of transactions,
number of customers--

00:16:23.160 --> 00:16:27.330
I can start to pull counts,
and sums, and averages

00:16:27.330 --> 00:16:31.080
on particular fields that are
coming from the Looker data

00:16:31.080 --> 00:16:34.200
model, customer
ID, and store ID.

00:16:34.200 --> 00:16:38.910
Or we can become a little more
complex with our aggregations

00:16:38.910 --> 00:16:41.280
by notating a measure
of type number

00:16:41.280 --> 00:16:43.560
and actually doing
the aggregation

00:16:43.560 --> 00:16:45.390
or doing the calculation
in the SQL itself.

00:16:47.970 --> 00:16:49.803
Now, to make it really
rich for end users,

00:16:49.803 --> 00:16:52.220
you'll notice on all of these
measures we have these drill

00:16:52.220 --> 00:16:53.870
detail fields.

00:16:53.870 --> 00:16:56.600
These drill details
will specify a list

00:16:56.600 --> 00:16:59.510
of fields that is pulled
back when a user clicks

00:16:59.510 --> 00:17:00.590
on that aggregation.

00:17:00.590 --> 00:17:03.720
And so you'll see drill
details defined up here.

00:17:03.720 --> 00:17:07.730
So if a user defines or
clicks on one of those metrics

00:17:07.730 --> 00:17:10.730
or measures, Looker is going
to uncover the date, the store

00:17:10.730 --> 00:17:14.000
name, the product
area, the product name

00:17:14.000 --> 00:17:15.680
so that the user can
see the real level

00:17:15.680 --> 00:17:17.630
detail related to that metric.

00:17:20.480 --> 00:17:22.280
Now, if our base
transaction table

00:17:22.280 --> 00:17:25.579
is an example of a core table
coming from our database

00:17:25.579 --> 00:17:29.570
connection, a couple of examples
of derive tables in Looker

00:17:29.570 --> 00:17:32.780
are our customer facts or
our customer transaction

00:17:32.780 --> 00:17:35.020
sequence table.

00:17:35.020 --> 00:17:36.770
So if we look at the
customer facts table,

00:17:36.770 --> 00:17:40.310
what you'll quickly notice
is we have the view name,

00:17:40.310 --> 00:17:42.380
but instead in the
SQL table name,

00:17:42.380 --> 00:17:44.847
we've invoked a derived table.

00:17:44.847 --> 00:17:46.430
And under the derived
table, it allows

00:17:46.430 --> 00:17:49.970
you to drop in a simple SQL
statement or a complex SQL

00:17:49.970 --> 00:17:54.140
statement so that you can start
to derive or prototype schema

00:17:54.140 --> 00:17:55.520
changes.

00:17:55.520 --> 00:17:57.020
What's powerful
about this is you

00:17:57.020 --> 00:18:00.020
don't need to physically copy
the data on the database side

00:18:00.020 --> 00:18:02.540
or create a fact table on
the database side in order

00:18:02.540 --> 00:18:04.640
for Looker to
leverage or for you

00:18:04.640 --> 00:18:07.970
to prototype what type of
analysis this would give you.

00:18:07.970 --> 00:18:13.030
You can simply create
that analysis in Looker.

00:18:13.030 --> 00:18:14.760
And if you'd like
to speed it up,

00:18:14.760 --> 00:18:20.280
we can start to persist
these pieces of data.

00:18:20.280 --> 00:18:23.420
So you'll notice here we've
got data group triggers.

00:18:23.420 --> 00:18:27.500
So this derived table is
governed by a caching policy.

00:18:27.500 --> 00:18:30.310
That's a daily caching policy.

00:18:30.310 --> 00:18:33.350
And a shortcut in Looker, if
I hold down the Option key,

00:18:33.350 --> 00:18:35.100
I'll get this plus button.

00:18:35.100 --> 00:18:37.370
I can click on that
data group trigger

00:18:37.370 --> 00:18:40.160
and Looker will navigate me
to where that's been defined.

00:18:40.160 --> 00:18:44.330
You'll notice it took me back to
my retail block modeling file,

00:18:44.330 --> 00:18:46.640
where our data groups
have been defined.

00:18:46.640 --> 00:18:49.340
In this case, our
daily data group

00:18:49.340 --> 00:18:52.160
is triggered by a
select current date.

00:18:52.160 --> 00:18:55.430
And the contingency
here is a max cache age.

00:18:55.430 --> 00:18:59.030
Why this is really powerful is,
if we want to sync Looker up

00:18:59.030 --> 00:19:00.920
with your data
pipeline-- let's say

00:19:00.920 --> 00:19:03.980
we want to sync Looker
with your ETL processes,

00:19:03.980 --> 00:19:07.520
we can define a SQL
trigger that detects

00:19:07.520 --> 00:19:10.580
changes in your ETL files.

00:19:10.580 --> 00:19:13.710
So maybe when a new ETL
process is completed,

00:19:13.710 --> 00:19:17.210
we can signify a rebuild
of that derived table,

00:19:17.210 --> 00:19:18.800
or a re-caching of that table.

00:19:23.310 --> 00:19:25.920
So going back to the
customer facts table,

00:19:25.920 --> 00:19:27.690
you'll also notice
a few pieces here--

00:19:27.690 --> 00:19:30.660
things like partition
keys and cluster keys.

00:19:30.660 --> 00:19:32.820
Now, when Looker is sitting
on top of a database,

00:19:32.820 --> 00:19:36.870
BigQuery or otherwise,
it is enhancing what

00:19:36.870 --> 00:19:38.380
is coming from the database.

00:19:38.380 --> 00:19:41.130
So given that we can
optimize BigQuery

00:19:41.130 --> 00:19:44.220
by utilizing partition
keys and cluster keys,

00:19:44.220 --> 00:19:48.360
Looker inherently has parameters
that we can use to leverage

00:19:48.360 --> 00:19:51.990
or define these partition keys
and cluster keys for folks that

00:19:51.990 --> 00:19:55.790
are defining derive tables.

00:19:55.790 --> 00:19:59.110
Now, for the derived table, we
can create this derived table

00:19:59.110 --> 00:20:01.550
by, instead of creating
view from table,

00:20:01.550 --> 00:20:03.820
creating a view outright.

00:20:03.820 --> 00:20:05.320
The other way we
can do it is we can

00:20:05.320 --> 00:20:08.210
use Looker to generate
the derived table for us.

00:20:08.210 --> 00:20:10.780
So a really powerful
work flow in Looker

00:20:10.780 --> 00:20:14.560
is to use the Explore section
to generate a SQL query.

00:20:14.560 --> 00:20:17.740
Now, for the end user, it will
always look like a result set.

00:20:17.740 --> 00:20:19.360
But for the more
technical developer,

00:20:19.360 --> 00:20:23.380
that SQL query is something that
you can use for your projects

00:20:23.380 --> 00:20:25.030
and models.

00:20:25.030 --> 00:20:28.180
So let's say this represented
a derived table that I would

00:20:28.180 --> 00:20:30.350
like to use for a project.

00:20:30.350 --> 00:20:31.907
I can open in SQL
runner down here

00:20:31.907 --> 00:20:33.240
in the bottom right-hand corner.

00:20:37.220 --> 00:20:40.990
And Looker will move my
query back to the SQL runner.

00:20:40.990 --> 00:20:45.580
SQL runner can be navigated to
here in our development tab.

00:20:45.580 --> 00:20:49.730
But once that query
is run, and I'll just

00:20:49.730 --> 00:20:51.980
take a look at the
results set here,

00:20:51.980 --> 00:20:54.350
I can go into the upper
right-hand corner,

00:20:54.350 --> 00:20:57.020
and I can derived table LookML.

00:20:57.020 --> 00:21:00.230
So I can already
derive that table

00:21:00.230 --> 00:21:02.580
and create the view
file for myself.

00:21:02.580 --> 00:21:05.450
You'll see that every column
coming from that view file

00:21:05.450 --> 00:21:07.370
or coming from that
SQL query has now been

00:21:07.370 --> 00:21:09.590
its own measure in dimension.

00:21:09.590 --> 00:21:13.750
And all I need to do is
add it back to my project.

00:21:13.750 --> 00:21:16.470
So you can use Looker to
expedite the SQL writing

00:21:16.470 --> 00:21:17.880
process for you.

00:21:17.880 --> 00:21:19.560
Even if you're writing
a derived table

00:21:19.560 --> 00:21:21.420
or a native derived
table, you can

00:21:21.420 --> 00:21:23.490
use Looker to help
you generate that SQL

00:21:23.490 --> 00:21:24.740
and generate that view file.

00:21:27.320 --> 00:21:31.580
Now, aside from the
customer facts table,

00:21:31.580 --> 00:21:36.490
we also have examples of BQML
being utilized in Looker.

00:21:36.490 --> 00:21:39.490
Our stock forecasting tables and
our customer clustering tables

00:21:39.490 --> 00:21:41.810
are two examples here.

00:21:41.810 --> 00:21:44.860
So if we start with the
stock forecasting table,

00:21:44.860 --> 00:21:47.860
you'll notice a little different
flavor of a derived table.

00:21:47.860 --> 00:21:50.810
Instead of a SQL
specific derived table,

00:21:50.810 --> 00:21:54.802
we're using something called a
native derived table in Looker.

00:21:54.802 --> 00:21:56.260
Why native drive
derived tables are

00:21:56.260 --> 00:22:01.090
powerful is, as you will notice,
they're not dialect specific.

00:22:01.090 --> 00:22:06.370
So LookML, as a modeling layer,
it is a SQL translation layer.

00:22:06.370 --> 00:22:10.450
The value there is that with any
point and click of an end user,

00:22:10.450 --> 00:22:12.250
Looker is translating
those clicks

00:22:12.250 --> 00:22:14.890
into optimize SQL
for whatever database

00:22:14.890 --> 00:22:17.880
we're sitting on top of.

00:22:17.880 --> 00:22:19.640
So in the case of
native derived tables,

00:22:19.640 --> 00:22:22.400
given that these are
not dialect specific,

00:22:22.400 --> 00:22:26.390
it makes it really easy if I'm
going through a data warehouse

00:22:26.390 --> 00:22:29.660
modernization project,
or I'm moving back ends

00:22:29.660 --> 00:22:31.520
and using Looker
as the front end

00:22:31.520 --> 00:22:33.500
to help me with that migration.

00:22:33.500 --> 00:22:37.250
I can simply swap out the
connection name in my model

00:22:37.250 --> 00:22:40.565
file, and I can reuse
these derived tables.

00:22:40.565 --> 00:22:42.815
I don't need to rewrite them
into a different dialect.

00:22:45.320 --> 00:22:48.710
So the stock forecasting
shows a nice example of that.

00:22:48.710 --> 00:22:52.040
Within the stock forecasting
view file-- and forgive me

00:22:52.040 --> 00:22:52.850
for my scrolling.

00:22:52.850 --> 00:22:55.520
I'm going to have to scroll
down to the bottom here--

00:22:55.520 --> 00:22:59.250
we'll be able to see
examples of our BQML model.

00:22:59.250 --> 00:23:01.670
So with the BQML model
here, we did feed

00:23:01.670 --> 00:23:06.500
in about 200 inputs, just
from our transactions table.

00:23:06.500 --> 00:23:08.510
And ultimately,
what we're doing is

00:23:08.510 --> 00:23:11.060
we're using BQML
linear regression

00:23:11.060 --> 00:23:14.800
to forecast sales by store,
by item, and by week.

00:23:14.800 --> 00:23:18.200
And so if we get down
here to the regression,

00:23:18.200 --> 00:23:22.130
you can see here that we're
using our SQL create statement

00:23:22.130 --> 00:23:27.260
on this derived table
to create or replace

00:23:27.260 --> 00:23:30.050
the name of that derived table--

00:23:30.050 --> 00:23:32.930
and using linear
regression here.

00:23:32.930 --> 00:23:35.580
Now, they're defined
in our LookML model,

00:23:35.580 --> 00:23:38.360
so if I want to change
our linear regression

00:23:38.360 --> 00:23:40.760
or iterate on some
of these BQML models,

00:23:40.760 --> 00:23:43.400
we can easily iterate
on those models,

00:23:43.400 --> 00:23:46.940
validate them, and test
them on the front end.

00:23:46.940 --> 00:23:49.550
You'll also notice down
here that we're also

00:23:49.550 --> 00:23:53.310
doing some sort of prediction on
our stock forecasting as well.

00:23:57.210 --> 00:23:59.450
Now, for customer
clustering, this

00:23:59.450 --> 00:24:03.590
is a really good example
of using clustering

00:24:03.590 --> 00:24:05.900
to create customer segments.

00:24:05.900 --> 00:24:08.720
So again, we have our customer
clustering input up here

00:24:08.720 --> 00:24:10.150
at the top, where
we're inputting

00:24:10.150 --> 00:24:12.770
a native derived table
coming from Looker.

00:24:12.770 --> 00:24:14.810
And then we can define
the clustering model here

00:24:14.810 --> 00:24:17.200
with k-means clustering.

00:24:17.200 --> 00:24:20.690
And we've defined
the clusters of four.

00:24:20.690 --> 00:24:23.990
And so with those clusters,
then on the front end,

00:24:23.990 --> 00:24:27.530
we can use our customers
explore to start

00:24:27.530 --> 00:24:31.850
to explore those different
customer segments,

00:24:31.850 --> 00:24:34.250
whether they are
millennials, Gen Xers--

00:24:34.250 --> 00:24:37.070
whether they're
net new customers.

00:24:37.070 --> 00:24:39.010
And you'll see down
here at the bottom

00:24:39.010 --> 00:24:42.720
that we also have a customer
clustering prediction.

00:24:42.720 --> 00:24:44.300
And so what this is doing is--

00:24:44.300 --> 00:24:47.390
this is out of the
box with our block

00:24:47.390 --> 00:24:50.360
to help cluster our
customers into those buckets,

00:24:50.360 --> 00:24:53.865
whether they're millennials,
retirees, or Gen Xers.

00:24:53.865 --> 00:24:55.240
Again, this can
be used right out

00:24:55.240 --> 00:24:58.730
of the box with
this retail block.

00:24:58.730 --> 00:25:00.560
Or, if you'd like to
iterate on this model

00:25:00.560 --> 00:25:02.990
or customize it in any way,
shape or form, you can.

00:25:07.000 --> 00:25:09.280
Now, the last piece
I wanted to touch on

00:25:09.280 --> 00:25:11.830
before we moved into
the Explore section

00:25:11.830 --> 00:25:13.970
was our public data sets
that we're working with.

00:25:13.970 --> 00:25:17.620
So if we look at our store
weather table here in Looker,

00:25:17.620 --> 00:25:22.150
this is an example where
we're using the BigQuery

00:25:22.150 --> 00:25:24.820
global daily weather
public data set

00:25:24.820 --> 00:25:29.770
to map a retailer stores to
the nearest weather stations.

00:25:29.770 --> 00:25:33.290
We want to get each store's
daily weather out of the box.

00:25:33.290 --> 00:25:35.860
And so you can see the
BigQuery public data

00:25:35.860 --> 00:25:39.190
set being leveraged here for all
different years and the mapping

00:25:39.190 --> 00:25:42.250
that's happening on Looker.

00:25:42.250 --> 00:25:48.043
So I do like to describe
the LookML model in Looker

00:25:48.043 --> 00:25:49.960
as kind of a lightweight
transformation layer.

00:25:49.960 --> 00:25:53.410
So you can see that some of
these models are very complex.

00:25:53.410 --> 00:25:55.150
You can host a lot
of complex logic.

00:25:55.150 --> 00:25:57.970
But more importantly,
I'm able to leverage,

00:25:57.970 --> 00:25:59.770
really, any function
or functionality

00:25:59.770 --> 00:26:04.600
that is coming from BigQuery
or GCP to make this really rich

00:26:04.600 --> 00:26:05.350
for our end users.

00:26:08.320 --> 00:26:09.790
Now, with all of those pieces--

00:26:09.790 --> 00:26:13.310
and I'll go ahead
and stay logged in--

00:26:13.310 --> 00:26:15.760
we want to navigate to
the explorers, right?

00:26:15.760 --> 00:26:18.070
So all of the work we've
done with our store-- weather

00:26:18.070 --> 00:26:22.390
views, all of our transaction
and our clustering.

00:26:22.390 --> 00:26:25.210
What we've done is we've
gone into our model file.

00:26:25.210 --> 00:26:27.730
And we've defined different
explores-- transaction

00:26:27.730 --> 00:26:31.270
detail, customer-- and we
have all of the joins defined.

00:26:31.270 --> 00:26:37.390
And so for myself as an end
user, what's really easy to do

00:26:37.390 --> 00:26:39.040
is to now move into
the Explore section

00:26:39.040 --> 00:26:42.520
and build any analysis
that I'd like.

00:26:42.520 --> 00:26:44.530
So if we go ahead into
the Explore section,

00:26:44.530 --> 00:26:49.080
I'm going to navigate back
to our transaction detail.

00:26:49.080 --> 00:26:51.450
And I'm actually going to
jump out of development mode.

00:26:55.910 --> 00:26:58.350
And so now, on my
transaction detail explorer,

00:26:58.350 --> 00:27:00.760
on the left-hand side,
everything that was joined

00:27:00.760 --> 00:27:03.890
are now tables that I
can point and click from.

00:27:03.890 --> 00:27:06.320
So if I look at our
transactions table,

00:27:06.320 --> 00:27:08.680
I can see I can
pivot, filter, or add

00:27:08.680 --> 00:27:10.510
some sort of a custom measure.

00:27:10.510 --> 00:27:13.930
If I'm a developer, I can move
backwards into the LookML.

00:27:13.930 --> 00:27:16.240
This is really
important, that Looker

00:27:16.240 --> 00:27:18.820
allows you to move
seamlessly from the front end

00:27:18.820 --> 00:27:20.650
to the back end.

00:27:20.650 --> 00:27:22.690
The architecture
being simplified

00:27:22.690 --> 00:27:25.690
as it is, given that all of your
metrics are governed under one

00:27:25.690 --> 00:27:26.830
develop tab--

00:27:26.830 --> 00:27:30.400
it's very easy to understand,
not only how things

00:27:30.400 --> 00:27:34.150
are defined, but
to update metrics

00:27:34.150 --> 00:27:36.790
and to update dimensions
for end users,

00:27:36.790 --> 00:27:40.505
to deliver the analysis
that they want.

00:27:40.505 --> 00:27:41.880
Now, along with
those dimensions,

00:27:41.880 --> 00:27:44.910
there are my dimension groups
with all of my type time.

00:27:44.910 --> 00:27:49.340
And down here at the bottom in
orange are all of my measures.

00:27:49.340 --> 00:27:52.430
Now, we do have
our access filter

00:27:52.430 --> 00:27:54.890
up here at the top, or our
filter for our partitions

00:27:54.890 --> 00:27:56.370
up here at the top.

00:27:56.370 --> 00:27:58.930
And so let's say I want to
do a year over year sample.

00:27:58.930 --> 00:28:01.520
Let's say I would like to
look at data for the past four

00:28:01.520 --> 00:28:02.430
years.

00:28:02.430 --> 00:28:06.350
So I'd like to do a year over
year for the past four years.

00:28:06.350 --> 00:28:09.910
I can go ahead and add my
month number into the analysis.

00:28:09.910 --> 00:28:14.220
I might want to look at my
total sales year over year.

00:28:14.220 --> 00:28:18.080
And I will pivot that
by my year bucket.

00:28:18.080 --> 00:28:20.360
And we can give that a run.

00:28:20.360 --> 00:28:22.830
Now, you might have noticed in
the upper right-hand corner,

00:28:22.830 --> 00:28:24.372
as I'm querying,
Looker will tell you

00:28:24.372 --> 00:28:25.730
whether it's pulling from cache.

00:28:25.730 --> 00:28:28.035
Or it will tell you
how large the results

00:28:28.035 --> 00:28:32.510
set-- it will predict the size
of the query before you run it.

00:28:32.510 --> 00:28:34.220
Now, when it returns
a visualization,

00:28:34.220 --> 00:28:37.068
I've got my visualization,
along with the data table.

00:28:37.068 --> 00:28:39.610
I can go ahead and start to play
with how this is visualized.

00:28:39.610 --> 00:28:42.560
So let's say I want to
not plot null values.

00:28:42.560 --> 00:28:47.030
And I'd like to add an outline
to my year over year analysis.

00:28:47.030 --> 00:28:49.310
Again, this is all
being generated off

00:28:49.310 --> 00:28:50.460
of that core model.

00:28:50.460 --> 00:28:54.130
So there are all of those
timezone conversions,

00:28:54.130 --> 00:28:57.020
the filters that I've added.

00:28:57.020 --> 00:28:59.040
In our SQL statement
for the end user,

00:28:59.040 --> 00:29:01.457
it's a simplified results set.

00:29:01.457 --> 00:29:03.040
As soon as it's
rendered, I can drill.

00:29:03.040 --> 00:29:07.210
If I want to look at all
354k on this data point,

00:29:07.210 --> 00:29:10.910
Looker will also automatically
handle time hierarchies.

00:29:10.910 --> 00:29:13.510
So if I want to continue to
drill down into different time

00:29:13.510 --> 00:29:15.570
hierarchies here, I can.

00:29:15.570 --> 00:29:18.490
But to support that
data analysis workflow,

00:29:18.490 --> 00:29:21.820
if I want to then add
this back to my dashboard

00:29:21.820 --> 00:29:24.790
or save it as a report, I can.

00:29:24.790 --> 00:29:26.770
I can also save and start
to schedule this out.

00:29:26.770 --> 00:29:29.320
So when we're talking
about integrated insights

00:29:29.320 --> 00:29:31.960
or data driven
workflows, what I can do

00:29:31.960 --> 00:29:34.810
is I can set those data driven
workflows here in the Explorer

00:29:34.810 --> 00:29:38.050
and start to push those out
to different users or groups--

00:29:38.050 --> 00:29:40.120
or set those workflows
in motion that

00:29:40.120 --> 00:29:42.790
are going to be most
beneficial to me.

00:29:42.790 --> 00:29:46.000
Now, if I go to save this
back to my dashboard,

00:29:46.000 --> 00:29:48.460
I'll go into my folder here.

00:29:48.460 --> 00:29:50.380
And we can save it back
to our group overview

00:29:50.380 --> 00:29:51.635
that we started on.

00:29:51.635 --> 00:29:53.635
And I'm going to call
this year over year sales.

00:29:57.090 --> 00:30:01.922
Now, we can head back to the
group overview dashboard.

00:30:01.922 --> 00:30:03.380
And this is where
the end users are

00:30:03.380 --> 00:30:07.820
going to come to consume all of
the stock forecasting, customer

00:30:07.820 --> 00:30:10.760
clustering, the public
data sets that we've

00:30:10.760 --> 00:30:13.370
laced into this demo.

00:30:13.370 --> 00:30:15.890
But now it's in easy
to consume format.

00:30:15.890 --> 00:30:16.670
It's visual.

00:30:16.670 --> 00:30:18.150
I can see my basket size.

00:30:18.150 --> 00:30:21.710
If I'm a VP of retail,
I can see the percent

00:30:21.710 --> 00:30:25.910
of customers that are
transacting, from my loyalty

00:30:25.910 --> 00:30:26.960
customers.

00:30:26.960 --> 00:30:30.390
I can see geographically
where my stores are located.

00:30:30.390 --> 00:30:33.020
And if I want to dig into
any sort of top movers,

00:30:33.020 --> 00:30:36.410
I can look at my change by
store and change by category.

00:30:36.410 --> 00:30:38.660
And I can easily start
to take action on these.

00:30:38.660 --> 00:30:41.390
So now if I want to integrate
with Google App Engine

00:30:41.390 --> 00:30:44.840
and create a text
or a call function

00:30:44.840 --> 00:30:48.500
to contact that
store manager, we

00:30:48.500 --> 00:30:50.990
can send a text quickly
to that store manager.

00:30:50.990 --> 00:30:54.830
I can go ahead and grab the
link that would hit their inbox.

00:30:54.830 --> 00:30:56.570
And when I open
that link, I'll be

00:30:56.570 --> 00:30:59.180
taken to a store deep dive
that's already been updated

00:30:59.180 --> 00:31:01.230
for my Los Angeles store.

00:31:01.230 --> 00:31:03.410
And so I can easily
start to take a look

00:31:03.410 --> 00:31:07.310
at why this store might be
under-performing-- and then

00:31:07.310 --> 00:31:09.230
look at different
examples of that work

00:31:09.230 --> 00:31:10.880
we did on the back
end and how it's

00:31:10.880 --> 00:31:12.530
displaying on the front end.

00:31:12.530 --> 00:31:16.100
So if we're looking at
our peer store comparison,

00:31:16.100 --> 00:31:19.160
I can see how I'm doing
versus my peer stores.

00:31:19.160 --> 00:31:21.290
I can look at the
weather trends to see

00:31:21.290 --> 00:31:25.070
if my transactions
decreasing has anything

00:31:25.070 --> 00:31:26.990
to do with the weather.

00:31:26.990 --> 00:31:29.210
And then when we get down
to stock forecasting here,

00:31:29.210 --> 00:31:32.270
I can easily see how
much of a product

00:31:32.270 --> 00:31:35.420
I have in stock versus
what's forecasted

00:31:35.420 --> 00:31:37.860
and what the stock
difference might be.

00:31:37.860 --> 00:31:40.550
And then if I need to take
action on any of these pieces,

00:31:40.550 --> 00:31:42.290
I can go ahead and
explore from here

00:31:42.290 --> 00:31:44.180
and move backwards into
the report building

00:31:44.180 --> 00:31:47.180
interface, where I can
start to alert when my stock

00:31:47.180 --> 00:31:48.920
difference, in
this case, might be

00:31:48.920 --> 00:31:53.140
greater than or equal
to a particular value.

00:31:53.140 --> 00:31:56.630
And of course, this is just
the BI and analytics workflow.

00:31:56.630 --> 00:31:58.360
So if we want to
get more advanced,

00:31:58.360 --> 00:32:00.190
certainly, this could
become an iframe

00:32:00.190 --> 00:32:03.970
that I then embed into
a custom application.

00:32:03.970 --> 00:32:07.810
Any of these points become API
endpoints or query endpoints

00:32:07.810 --> 00:32:13.870
that I can pull into a custom
application or custom workflow.

00:32:13.870 --> 00:32:16.960
But that's the end of my
technical deep dive on Looker.

00:32:16.960 --> 00:32:21.570
I hope that it was informative,
and thanks so much for joining.

